    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12.x\n\n### Description\n\nGiven the following XML\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<pkg>\n  <foo-bar>foobar</foo-bar>\n</pkg>\n```\n\n`mini.ai` is incorrectly ignoring `<foo-bar>` as a tag, pressumably because of the hyphen in the name. See https://www.w3.org/TR/2008/REC-xml-20081126/#NT-NameChar for the specification.\n\nBoth treesitter (through `:InspectTree`) and a clean neovim configuration (`nvim -u NONE`) are correctly matching this inner entry, while `mini.ai` is overriding this.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nThe configuration that causes this is fairly minimal:\n\n```lua\nlocal function gh(repo) return 'https://github.com/' .. repo end\n\nvim.pack.add { gh 'nvim-mini/mini.nvim' }\nrequire('mini.ai').setup {}\n```\n\nIf `require('mini.ai').setup {}` is removed, everything works as expected.\n\nIdeally I would be able to fix this through \n```lua\nlocal ts = require('mini.ai').gen_spec.treesitter\nrequire('mini.ai').setup {\n  custom_textobjects = {\n    t = ts { a = '@tag.outer', i = '@tag.inner' },\n  }\n}\n```\nbut nvim-treesitter-textobjects doesn't support XML currently.",
